¶
'''2.''' A timepiece used as a time-detector or time-reporter for a watchman. {{smaller|It is made in many forms. One kind is a small portable clock that must be carried by the watchman to different stations on his rounds. At each station a special key fastened to a chain must be used to make a mark on a paper dial inside the clock, thus making a record of the performance of his duty. Another form consists of a fixed clock, having a key that must be touched to make the record, a clock being placed at each station. Another and now more common form is a clock placed at a central station, and connected by wires with the place where the watchman makes his rounds; at each station the watchman touches a push-button to close the circuit and print a mark on a dial in the clock.}}

'''watch-dog''' (woch′dog), ''n.'' A dog kept to watch or guard premises and property.
{{smaller block|<poem>’Tis sweet to hear the ''watch-dog’s'' honest bark
Bay deep-mouth’d welcome as we draw near home.</poem>
{{right|''Byron'', Don Juan, i. 123.}}}}

'''watcher''' (woch′ėr), ''n.'' One who or that which watches. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') One who sits up and continues awake; one who lies awake.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Get on your nightgown, lest occasion call us,
And show us to be ''watchers''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, ii. 2. 71.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') One who keeps awake for the purpose of guarding or attending upon something or some one; a nurse, watchman, sentry, or the like.}}
{{smaller block|On the fronters . . . were set watchmen and ''watchers'' in dyuers manners.
{{right|''Berners'', tr. of Froissart’s Chron., II. xlix.}}
<poem>A charr’d and wrinkled piece of womanhood
Sat watching like a ''watcher'' by the dead.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, v.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') One who observes: as, a ''watcher'' of the time.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Then felt I like some ''watcher'' of the skies,
When a new planet swims into his ken.</poem>}}

