'''watch-tackle''' (woch′tak″l), ''n.'' ''Naut.'', a small tackle consisting of a double and single block with a fall. Also called ''handy-billy.''
{{smaller block|By hauling every brace and bowline, and clapping ''watch-tackles'' upon all the sheets and halyards, we managed to hold our own.
{{right|''R. H. Dana, Jr.'', Before the Mast, p. 250.}}}}

'''watch-telescope''' (woch′tel″e-skōp), ''n.'' See ''telescope.''

'''watch-tower''' (woch′tou″ėr), ''n.'' A tower on which a sentinel is placed to watch for enemies, for the approach of danger, etc.
{{smaller block|I stand continually upon the ''watch-tower'' in the day-time.
{{right|Isa. xxi. 8.}}
About a mile from the towne there is a very high and strong ''watch tower.''
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 10.}}}}

'''watchword''' (woch′wėrd), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wacche-word''; < ''watch'' + ''word.''] '''1.''' A word or short phrase to be communicated on challenge to the watch or sentinels in a camp; a password or signal by which friends can be known from enemies.
{{smaller block|''Wacche wordes'' to wale, that weghis might know.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 6056.}}}}
Hence—'''2.''' Any preconcerted indication or a direction eagerly watched for, as a signal for action.
{{smaller block|All have theyr eares upright, wayting when the ''watch-woord'' shall come that they should all rise generally into rebellion.
{{right|''Spenser'', State of Ireland.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
'''3.''' A word used as a motto, as expressive of a principle or rule of action; a maxim, byword, or rallying-cry.
{{smaller block|“Now” is the constant syllable ticking from the clock of time. “Now” is the ''watchword'' of the wise. “Now” is on the banner of the prudent. {{right|''Parr.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>His ''watchword'' is honour, his pay is renown.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Rokeby, v. 20.}}}}

'''4†.''' The call of a watchman or sentry as he goes his rounds.
{{smaller block|Since when a ''watchword'' every minute of the night goeth about the wals to testifie their vigilancy.
{{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 10.}}}}

'''To set a watchword upon†''', {{smaller|to make proverbial; turn into a byword.}}
{{smaller block|S. Paule himselfe (who yet for the credite of Poets) alledgeth twise two Poets, . . . ''setteth a watch-word vpon'' Philosophy, indeede vpon the abuse. So dooth Plato, vpon the abuse, not vpon Poetrie. Plato found fault that the Poet of his time filled the worlde with wrong opinions of the Gods. {{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Apol. for Poetrie.}}}}

'''watchwork''' (woch′wėrk), ''n.'' The machinery of a watch: now usually in the plural.

'''wate†''', ''v. t.'' A form of ''wat''<sup>1</sup>. See ''wit''<sup>1</sup>.

'''water''' (wâ′tėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''water'', ''watre'', ''wæter'', ''weter'', < AS. ''wæter'' = OS. ''watar'' = OFries. ''weter'', ''water'' = D. ''water'' = MLG. ''water'' = OHG. ''wazzar'', MHG. ''wazzer'', G. ''wasser'', water; with a formative ''-r'', akin to Icel. ''vatn'' = Sw. ''vatten'' = Dan. ''vand'' = Goth. ''watō'' (pl. ''watnō''), in which a different formative ''-n'' appears; cf. OBulg. Russ. ''voda'', Lith. ''wandů'', Gr. ὕδωρ (ὑδατ-, ὑδρ-), Skt. ''udan'', water; < Teut. √ ''wat'', Indo-Eur. √ ''wad'', be wet. Cf. ''wash'', perhaps from the same root as ''water''. See ''wet''<sup>1</sup>.]
